<article class="post-body">

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §1 The Discrete Gap -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Discrete Gap</div>
<h2>Why language and diffusion fight each other</h2>
<p>
  Diffusion models thrive in continuous spaces. Given a pixel image, you corrupt it with Gaussian noise and train a network to predict the clean image — the whole pipeline is smooth, differentiable, and mathematically elegant. Language is <strong>not</strong> continuous. A sentence is a sequence of discrete integer indices into a vocabulary. There is no sensible "halfway between 'cat' and 'dog'".
</p>
<p>
  The field resolved this tension in two ways:
</p>

<div class="emb-grid">
  <div class="emb-cell">
    <div class="emb-cell-title">Discrete DLMs</div>
    <div class="emb-cell-score score-ok">Works</div>
    <div class="emb-cell-desc">Diffuse directly over token indices. Use masking or categorical corruption. Conceptually clean but can't use continuous techniques like CFG.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="emb-cell">
    <div class="emb-cell-title">Continuous DLMs (prior)</div>
    <div class="emb-cell-score score-bad">Lagged</div>
    <div class="emb-cell-desc">Map tokens → embeddings, denoise in that space. But used per-step token losses that tied the trajectory back to discrete tokens at every step, limiting flexibility.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="emb-cell">
    <div class="emb-cell-title">ELF</div>
    <div class="emb-cell-score score-good">Wins</div>
    <div class="emb-cell-desc">Continuous-only denoising from start to finish. Discretization happens exactly once — at the final step — using the same network weights.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>
  The key failure mode of previous continuous DLMs: they kept pulling the trajectory back toward discrete token space at every step with a cross-entropy loss. This is like trying to drive a smooth road but stopping every 100 meters to snap your GPS back to the nearest house. The trajectory never flows freely.
</p>

<div class="callout callout-insight">
  <span class="callout-icon">💡</span>
  <span><strong>The ELF insight:</strong> Discretization is not a per-step nuisance to be managed — it is a <em>natural endpoint</em> of the flow. The final time step t=1 already wants to produce clean data, so repurpose it as your decoder. No extra components needed.</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §2 Flow Matching -->
<section id="s-flow" data-code="flow">
<div class="section-no">02 — Flow Matching</div>
<h2>From Gaussian noise to clean embeddings</h2>
<p>
  Flow Matching is the continuous-time successor to DDPM-style diffusion. Instead of thinking about T discrete denoising steps, you parameterize a smooth <em>velocity field</em> that moves a particle from noise at t=0 to data at t=1.
</p>

<h3>Rectified flows: the simplest path</h3>
<p>
  ELF uses the rectified flow interpolant — the straight-line path between noise and data:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Linear interpolation (rectified flow)</div>
  <span class="math-var">z</span><sub>t</sub> <span class="math-op">= t · </span><span class="math-var">x</span> <span class="math-op">+ (1 − t) · </span><span class="math-var">ε</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">  where x ~ data distribution, ε ~ N(0, I), t ∈ [0, 1]</span>
  <br><br>
  <span class="math-var">v</span> <span class="math-op">= dz/dt = </span><span class="math-var">x</span> <span class="math-op">− </span><span class="math-var">ε</span>
  <span class="math-comment">  ← constant velocity along the straight-line path</span>
</div>

<p>
  At t=0 you have pure Gaussian noise. At t=1 you have the clean embedding. In between, you're at a linear mixture. The network is trained to predict <strong>x</strong> (the clean embedding) at every point along this path, then velocity is derived from x-prediction via <code>v = (x̂ − z) / (1 − t)</code>.
</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">flow-trajectories.canvas</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">animated — press play</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="flow-canvas"></canvas>
    <div class="flow-controls">
      <button class="flow-btn" id="flow-play">▶ Play</button>
      <button class="flow-btn" id="flow-reset">↺ Reset</button>
      <span class="flow-t-label">t = <span id="flow-t-val">0.00</span></span>
      <span class="flow-t-label" style="margin-left:auto;font-size:.65rem;opacity:.7">Each dot = one token embedding trajectory</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<h3>Why x-prediction instead of v-prediction?</h3>
<p>
  Standard Flow Matching predicts velocity <strong>v</strong> directly. ELF predicts the clean embedding <strong>x</strong> instead, and derives v from it. This matters for two reasons:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Stability in high dimensions</strong> — token embeddings are 512-768 dimensional. Predicting the target directly is more stable than predicting the direction of travel.</li>
  <li><strong>Weight sharing</strong> — the final step needs to produce a clean embedding that maps to a token. If the network predicts x at all steps, the final-step decode mode and the denoising mode share the same output space, making weight sharing natural and effective.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-tip">
  <span class="callout-icon">⚡</span>
  <span>The MSE loss in x-prediction space is: <code>L = E[(1/(1-t))² · ‖x̂θ(zₜ,t) − x‖²]</code>. The (1/(1-t))² weight upweights errors near t=1, where the model must be most precise.</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §3 ELF Framework -->
