<h3>The encoder: contextual embeddings matter</h3>
<p>
  ELF encodes the input sequence with a pretrained T5-small encoder (frozen). This gives <strong>contextual</strong> embeddings — each token's representation considers its full sentence context. This is crucial: ELF's ablations show contextual embeddings beat non-contextual (single lookup table) embeddings by a large margin.
</p>
<p>
  Crucially, the encoder is only needed at <strong>training time</strong> to produce clean target embeddings x. At inference, you start from Gaussian noise and denoise toward the learned embedding distribution — the encoder never runs.
</p>

<h3>The bottleneck projection</h3>
<p>
  T5-small produces 512-dimensional embeddings per token. ELF projects these down to 128-dim for the flow, then back up to match the denoiser's hidden size. This bottleneck (from the "Back to Basics" paper by Tianhong Li and Kaiming He — same group!) is a design choice that stabilizes the flow dynamics in high-dimensional spaces.
</p>

<div class="loss-split">
  <div class="loss-mse">80% MSE loss (denoising, t &lt; 1)</div>
  <div class="loss-ce">20% CE loss (decode, t = 1)</div>
</div>
<p style="font-size:.8rem;color:var(--muted);">Training batch composition — 80% of samples use the denoising branch (MSE), 20% use the decoding branch (cross-entropy). Both branches share the same network weights.</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §4 Final Step Trick -->
<section id="s-final" data-code="final">
<div class="section-no">04 — The Final Step Trick</div>
<h2>Why you only need to discretize once</h2>
<p>
  This is the core idea that makes ELF work. In Flow Matching, t=1 is where z<sub>t</sub> should equal the clean data x. So when the ODE integrator reaches t=1, the network input is approximately the clean embedding. Discretization is just <em>reading off the nearest token from the embedding</em>.
</p>
<p>
  But there is a training subtlety: at t=1 exactly, z₁ = x, which means the network input carries no uncertainty — the task is trivially easy and the model learns nothing about decoding. ELF's fix: at the decoding branch, corrupt x with a small amount of noise to create z̃, giving the network a non-trivial input while still training it to produce discrete logits.
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Final-step decode path</div>
  <span class="math-comment">Training (decoding branch):</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-var">z̃</span> <span class="math-op">= corrupt(</span><span class="math-var">x</span><span class="math-op">)</span>
  <span class="math-comment">  ← nontrivial input via masking/noise</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-var">x̂</span> <span class="math-op">= netθ(</span><span class="math-var">z̃</span><span class="math-op">, t=1, mode="decode")</span>
  <br>
  loss <span class="math-op">= CrossEntropy(W · </span><span class="math-var">x̂</span><span class="math-op">, s)</span>
  <span class="math-comment">  ← W = unembedding matrix</span>
  <br><br>
  <span class="math-comment">Inference (final step only):</span>
  <br>
  tokens <span class="math-op">= argmax(W · netθ(</span><span class="math-var">z</span><sub>1</sub><span class="math-op">, t=1, mode="decode"))</span>
</div>

<h3>Shared weights: the elegant shortcut</h3>
<p>
  The denoiser and the decoder share all weights. A single binary "mode" token in the network's input tells it which task to perform. This is better than a two-stage pipeline (separate decoder) because:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>The denoiser already learns rich representations of clean embeddings — the decoder just needs to project them to tokens.</li>
  <li>No additional training stage, no additional parameters at inference.</li>
  <li>The shared training signal keeps the embedding space aligned with what the unembedding layer expects.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-insight">
  <span class="callout-icon">💡</span>
  <span>ELF's ablation confirms: the shared-weight variant extends further toward very low generative perplexity than a separately trained decoder. Sharing constraints the network to maintain a consistent embedding geometry throughout the flow.</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §5 CFG & Guidance -->
<section id="s-guidance" data-code="guidance">
<div class="section-no">05 — CFG & Guidance</div>
<h2>Getting continuous guidance for free</h2>
<p>
  Classifier-Free Guidance (CFG) is one of the most powerful tools in image diffusion — it lets you trade diversity for quality by blending conditional and unconditional predictions. For language, it has been "largely unexplored and shown less effective" in discrete DLMs, precisely because CFG requires interpolating in a continuous velocity space.
</p>
<p>
  Since ELF operates entirely in continuous embedding space, CFG applies directly:
</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">CFG velocity blending</div>
  <span class="math-var">v</span><sub>cfg</sub><span class="math-op">(zₜ | c) = ω · v(zₜ | c) + (1 − ω) · v(zₜ | ∅)</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">  ω = guidance scale; c = condition; ∅ = null condition</span>
  <br>
  <span class="math-comment">  ω > 1 amplifies the conditional direction → lower PPL, less diversity</span>
</div>

<h3>Self-conditioning: making guidance work without labels</h3>
<p>
  Standard CFG requires a class label or text prompt as condition c. For unconditional text generation, ELF uses <strong>self-conditioning</strong>: the prediction from the previous time step x̂′ becomes the condition for the current step. This is implemented by concatenating [z<sub>t</sub>, x̂′] as the network input — zero overhead at inference since you already have the previous prediction.
</p>

<div class="cfg-viz">
  <canvas id="cfg-canvas"></canvas>
</div>
<div class="cfg-legend">
  <div class="cfg-leg-item"><div class="cfg-dot" style="background:#5c6370"></div>ω=0 (unconditional)</div>
  <div class="cfg-leg-item"><div class="cfg-dot" style="background:#61afef"></div>ω=1 (no guidance)</div>
  <div class="cfg-leg-item"><div class="cfg-dot" style="background:#c678dd"></div>ω=2 (light guidance)</div>
  <div class="cfg-leg-item"><div class="cfg-dot" style="background:#e5c07b"></div>ω=3 (strong guidance)</div>
  <div class="cfg-leg-item"><div class="cfg-dot" style="background:#98c379"></div>data distribution</div>
</div>

<h3>Training-time CFG</h3>
<p>
  The standard CFG approach doubles inference compute (two forward passes per step). ELF avoids this by using <strong>training-time CFG</strong> (following the Mean Flows technique from the same MIT group): the network is trained to directly produce the guided output x<sub>cfg</sub> in a single pass, modeling the CFG output distribution rather than combining two predictions at inference time.
</p>
</section>

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────── §6 Inference Walk -->
<section id="s-inference" data-code="inference">
<div class="section-no">06 — Inference Walk</div>
<h2>From noise to text: step by step</h2>
<p>
  At inference time, ELF runs a numerical ODE (or SDE) solver over the flow trajectory. Walk through each step below:
</p>

<div class="infer-steps" id="infer-steps">
  <div class="infer-step active" id="step-0">
    <div class="step-num">1</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Initialize: sample Gaussian noise</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Draw z₀ ~ N(0, I) of shape [L × d_emb]. This is your starting point — pure random noise with no structure at all.</div>
      <div class="step-code">z = randn(L, d_emb)   <span style="color:var(--muted);"># e.g. shape [1024, 128]</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="infer-step" id="step-1">
    <div class="step-num">2</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Iterative denoising loop (t: 0 → ~0.99)</div>
      <div class="step-desc">For each time step tᵢ in the schedule, run the ELF network in denoise mode to predict the clean embedding x̂, then convert to velocity and take an Euler step.</div>
      <div class="step-code">x_pred = net(z, t, mode="denoise")<br>v = (x_pred - z) / (1 - t)<br>z = z + dt * v</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="infer-step" id="step-2">
    <div class="step-num">3</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Optional: SDE noise injection per step</div>
      <div class="step-desc">The SDE variant injects a small amount of fresh noise at each step, resetting accumulated errors. This dramatically reduces generative perplexity in the few-step regime (32 steps).</div>
      <div class="step-code">z = z + dt * v + σ * randn_like(z)  <span style="color:var(--muted);"># SDE variant</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="infer-step" id="step-3">
    <div class="step-num">4</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Final step: switch to decode mode</div>
      <div class="step-desc">At the last step t=1, the network switches to "decode" mode. It produces clean embedding predictions which are projected to vocabulary logits by the unembedding matrix W.</div>
      <div class="step-code">h = net(z, t=1, mode="decode")<br>logits = unembed(h)  <span style="color:var(--muted);"># W @ h</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="infer-step" id="step-4">
    <div class="step-num">5</div>
    <div class="step-body">
      <div class="step-title">Discretize: argmax to tokens</div>
      <div class="step-desc">Apply argmax over the vocabulary dimension at each position to obtain the final discrete token sequence. All L tokens are produced simultaneously — generation is non-autoregressive.</div>
      <div class="step-code">tokens = argmax(logits, dim=-1)  <span style="color:var(--muted);"># shape [L]</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="infer-nav">
  <button id="infer-prev" disabled>‹ Prev</button>
  <button id="infer-next">Next ›</button>
  <span class="infer-progress" id="infer-prog">Step 1 of 5</span>
</div>

<h3>ODE vs SDE: when does stochasticity help?</h3>
<p>
  ELF's SDE-inspired sampler substantially outperforms the ODE in the few-step regime (8–32 steps), but they converge at high step counts. The intuition: the ODE deterministically follows the learned velocity field — small errors accumulate. The SDE's noise injection periodically "resets" these errors, trading a small accuracy cost per step for better error correction overall.
</p>
</section>

