<h3>What the ablations reveal</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Contextual embeddings win.</strong> Pretrained T5 encoder >> random encoder >> frozen Gaussian embeddings >> learnable embeddings. Context helps the flow model navigate the embedding space.</li>
  <li><strong>Shared weights win narrowly.</strong> A separately-trained two-stage decoder performs comparably overall, but the shared-weight variant achieves lower generative perplexity at the extreme end of the quality-diversity trade-off.</li>
  <li><strong>SDE beats ODE at ≤64 steps.</strong> At 128+ steps the gap closes. For practical deployment (fast generation), SDE is the clear choice.</li>
  <li><strong>CFG scale 2-3 is the sweet spot.</strong> Higher scales push perplexity down but also collapse diversity. The paper plots quality vs. entropy Pareto curves across CFG scales.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-insight">
  <span class="callout-icon">📊</span>
  <span><strong>The data efficiency story:</strong> ELF uses 45B training tokens versus 550B+ for prior DLMs — a 12× reduction. The paper attributes this partly to the Flow Matching formulation and partly to the pretrained T5 encoder providing a high-quality embedding space from day one.</span>
</div>

<h3>Scaling</h3>
<p>
  ELF scales predictably: ELF-B (105M) → ELF-M (342M) → ELF-L (652M) each push the generative perplexity–entropy Pareto frontier in the right direction. Larger models achieve lower perplexity at matched diversity, suggesting standard scaling laws apply.
</p>
</section>

